3-2 S-Unit Interface

In general, the inside of a CPU is divided into a insn control unit
and a memory (or cache) control unit.  In STM, we call them I-unit (IU)
and S-unit (SU), respectively.  (Now you know what my background is:-)

The interface between IU and SU needs some sort of handshaking.  Here 
we will see typical behaviors of load and store insns.

A) Load (or Mem-op) insn

In a load or mem-op insn, IU sends the memory (or cache) access request
signal to SU in A stage.  SU supplies a ready signal, which tells whether a 
request can be accepted or not in the current cycle.  When RDY is on, the
req is accepted.  When off, IU will stall in A stage.  This is called
SU-Busy (SBS) interlock.

If the memory data is available, SU returns the data and the cache-line-hit
(CLH) signal in B stage.  IU will proceed to E stage, and, with CLH returned,
IU will use the data for the execution (in mem-op insn) in E stage and
proceed to W stage. 

(Since the current design does not implement cache, CLH is just a random
signal or, optionally, always set to one in logic siml.  If it had cache, 
the cache tag would be read and checked for a match in B stage, and CLH
would give the result of the check.)

If cache miss occurs, i.e. CLH is off, IU still proceeds to E stage.  IU
latches CLH in B stage, and will stall in E stage if it detects the absence
of CLH in the previous B cycle.  This is called cache-line-miss (CLM)
interlock.  When, after some cycles, CLH becomes on, IU is allowed to
proceed.

When such a cache miss occurs, it is possible that IU sends a second 
request while SU is still dealing with a previous request.  To handle such
cases, SU has a WAIT request buffer, where SU temporarily stores the
second request.  While WAIT buffer has a valid entity, SU turns off the RDY
signal described above, inhibiting IU to send a third request.  As SU
sends the next CLH, the waiting req becomes ready for next memory-read
operation.  See the diagram below.

