B) Store insn

We will consider a store insn next.  It issues a req and waits for a CLH
in A stage, just like a load does.  Current design assumes a copy-back
cache of fetch-on-write type; with a cache miss on store, the corresponding
cache line will be fetched first.  The store data is read from the reg
file in B stage of IU.  (BTW, the RF port used for store data is the same
one as that for the reg operand of a mem-op insn, thus reducing the 
number of RF ports.)

Because of a possible interruption, the store data should not actually be
written into memory before W stage.  When a latched CLH is detected in
E stage, store data is put in the store buffer.

The number of store buffer is one of the important parameters in processor
design.  The current design  has just set it to one.  Maybe this is one point
that should be improved later.

Once the data is put in the store buffer, it is already *logically* stored
into memory; it will be reflected to memory even if an interruption occurs.
For an actual writing, IU sends another request to SU.  At this time, the
cache line is guaranteed to hit because it was fetched in the former
request.  Therefore no need to wait for CLH.  (When we design a cache for
multi-processor configuration in the future, we must make sure that a
request for the corresponding line from another CPU is made to wait until
this second request is accepted and executed.)

So we have 3 kinds of requests; load, store 1st, and store 2nd.  SU can
accept at most one per clock cycle.

Assume that there are 4 consecutive insns, X, Y, Z and U, that X and Y
are stores, and that Z and U are either load, store, or mem-op (i.e.
issue requests).  They go through pipeline without interlock nor
simultaneous issue, and they are now in W, E, B, A stages, respectively.
Now X sends a store-2 request and U sends another request.  If SU decided
to accept U's req, X couldn't leave the store buffer, Y couldn't proceed
to W stage, Z couldn't proceed to E stage, and U couldn't proceed
either.  This is pointless, so in such cases SU accepts X's req.

If Y and Z are non-memory-use insns and all others being the same, Y and
Z, and therefore U, can proceed, and X's req may be accepted in the next
cycle hoping that no req is sent out of the next A stage.  For a more 
effective usage of SU pipeline, U's req is accepted in such cases.

If a store X is in W stage, another store Y is in E stage, B stage is a
bubble (no insn), and the insn in A stage is sending a request, X can't
leave the store buffer and hence Y cannot proceed.  So Y stalls in E
stage.  This is called a store buffer wait (STB) interlock.

